            content: "<p>Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi secured a landslide victory in parliamentary elections held on February 8, 2026, after previously dissolving parliament and calling a snap election on January 19, 2026. Her Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) secured a supermajority of more than two-thirds of parliamentary seats, a historic achievement making Takaichi the first woman elected as Japan's Prime Minister through a decisive election victory.</p><p>This victory grants Takaichi a strong mandate to continue her policy agenda, including strengthening national defense amid geopolitical pressure from China as well as economic reforms to address inflation challenges and yen weakness continuing to burden Japanese purchasing power.</p><p>Asian political observers believe this landslide victory also gives Takaichi greater room in diplomatic relations with China, which had previously reportedly considered changing its pressure approach toward Japan should Takaichi achieve a convincing election win.</p><p>Despite gaining strong domestic political support, Takaichi still faces significant foreign policy challenges, including her decision not to attend the NATO summit in July 2026 — a move drawing attention given Japan has been routinely invited to attend the forum since Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022.</p>",
