            desc: 'A series of sequential earthquakes struck northeastern Japan in late June 2026, followed by the threat of two simultaneous typhoons forcing the evacuation of over a million residents across central Honshu.',
            content: "<p>Japan faced a series of natural disasters in late June 2026, beginning with a magnitude 6.9 earthquake striking off the northeastern coast of Japan on June 25, followed by aftershocks that also rattled the Tokyo region on June 26. The Japan Meteorological Agency (JMA) even issued high-level landslide warnings across wide areas due to the earthquake sequence.</p><p>Shortly after the earthquake sequence, Japan also had to prepare for the threat of two simultaneous typhoons — Typhoon No. 7 and No. 8 — expected to hit central Honshu in close succession. Tokaido Shinkansen bullet train service was temporarily suspended as a safety precaution ahead of the double storm's arrival.</p><p>Local authorities reported over a million residents had been evacuated from disaster-prone areas along coastlines and mountainous regions at risk of landslides. This situation marked one of the rare large-scale evacuation operations occurring in close proximity to a significant earthquake sequence.</p><p>Japan's 2026 typhoon season itself is projected to be more active than usual, with weather agency Weathernews forecasting about 28 storms to form throughout the year — a figure above historical average, heightening authorities' and residents' vigilance toward potential compound natural disasters in coming months.</p>",
